 Each axis runs in its own sub-agent context so findings from one axis do not pollute another.
 
-**Size threshold:** diffs under ~30 changed lines get ONE reviewer sub-agent running all three passes sequentially in a single context; larger diffs get three parallel sub-agents. Axes, severities, and labels are identical either way — only the parallelism scales. (Data basis: parallel isolation paid for itself on feature-sized diffs and found nothing extra on tiny ones.)
+**Size threshold:** diffs under ~30 changed lines get ONE reviewer sub-agent running all three passes sequentially in a single context. Diffs under ~200 changed lines **in one layer** (a UI restyle, a repository-only change) also get ONE reviewer — on that size the axes are not independent: the same duplicated prop or CSS rule arrives three times under three labels. Three parallel sub-agents are for diffs that cross layers or exceed ~200 lines. Axes, severities, and labels are identical either way — only the parallelism scales. (Data basis: parallel isolation paid for itself on feature-sized diffs and found nothing extra on tiny ones.)
 
